  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface observations late this morning show widespread overcast\nstratus across the region. Analysis of the 12Z Wilmington OH \nsounding and 12Z model soundings across Charleston and \nClarksburg indicates a shallow moist layer trapped below a sharp\nsubsidence inversion near H850, where temperatures warm to \naround -1C to -3C. Because cloud tops remain warmer than -6C and\nwell below the dendritic growth zone, ice crystal growth is \nabsent across the lowlands, resulting in only spotty drizzle \nthis afternoon that will gradually fade as synoptic subsidence \nstrengthens. \n\nAcross the Northeast Mountains, northwesterly low-level flow of\n15 to 25KT between H900 and H800 continues to tap a narrow Lake\nHuron to Lake Erie moisture fetch. This trajectory will briefly\ngraze the higher terrain between 4 PM and 8 PM EST, supporting \nisolated to scattered rain showers in the mountain valleys and \nsnow showers along the highest ridges above 3000 ft where \nsurface temperatures hover near 30F to 32F. Parcel trajectories \nveer more northerly overnight as surface high pressure over the \nMidwest shifts eastward, cutting off the Great Lakes moisture \nsource and ending precipitation by late evening with little to \nno accumulation expected. \n\nTemperatures for this afternoon were decreased over central\nguidance with overnight temperatures increased slightly due to\npoor performance of central guidance bias correction in \novercast conditions. \n\nHeading into Monday and through the remainder of the work week,\nexpansive surface high pressure and building mid-level ridging\nwill establish a prolonged period of dry, tranquil weather.\nForecast soundings show an exceptionally dry air mass settling\noverhead through at least Wednesday, with H850 dew points\ndropping to -15C to -25C and H700 dew points below -25C. \nBecause central guidance dew points carry a notable high bias \nduring dry shoulder- season regimes, afternoon dew points were \nlowered into the 20s and lower 30s for Monday through Wednesday \nto capture daytime mixing into this dry air aloft. Despite lower\nafternoon relative humidity, light winds under 10 mph and moist\nantecedent 10-hour fuel moistures of 12 to 27 percent across \nregional RAWS sites will keep fire weather concerns minimal. \ninitially. Daytime highs will steadily moderate from the upper \n40s and lower 50s Monday into the upper 50s and lower 60s by mid\nto late week. \n\nThe next realistic chance for precipitation holds off until \nnext weekend as the ridge shifts east and a frontal system \napproaches from the Plains.",
